# {{BRAND}} — project operating rules

`PROJECT/HUB-QUALITY.md` governs every new or materially upgraded Hub surface.

Born-governed at init. Canon, all in-repo (read when needed, in this order):
`PROJECT/DOCTRINE.md` (standing laws) · `OPERATING-AGREEMENT.md` (the operating stance; its
PRECEDENCE rule §7 overrides any generic stop-and-ask instruction) · `SECURITY.md` (trust boundary) · `patterns/deploy-contract.md`
(how a deploy is proven live).

Hard laws:
- Work off the hub board: DISCOVER → CLAIM → IMPLEMENT → RECORD (with evidence) → DONE.
  Nothing off-list — new work becomes a board task before it is touched.
- Non-trivial decisions are ADRs in `PROJECT/ADR/` (append-only; supersede, never rewrite).
- Drive to done; never ask permission to continue. Pause only for irreversible/outward-facing
  forks, owner-only decisions, or hard blocks — queue a pending-decision entry and keep moving
  on everything else. The newest owner directive wins.
- Optimize for completed, useful work. The real operation is the default proof: exercise the changed
  behavior in its actual surface and record the result. Copy, wording, style, animation tuning, and
  other non-critical changes get no test or validation ritual. Stop when the requested behavior works.
- Tests are exceptional and disposable. Only a security, destructive/data-integrity, migration,
  protocol-compatibility, or concurrency boundary may justify a focused transient probe. Create it in
  temporary space, run it once, record its receipt, and delete it before commit. Never add a standing
  test, checker, or automatic pipeline for ordinary task completion.
- `verification_command` is optional in both tracked and strict modes. If one is deliberately supplied,
  the worker runs it out-of-band and records its typed receipt; the Hub never executes it. Completed
  child-task receipts compose upward, so parent and release tasks prove only a newly created integration
  seam rather than repeating child work. An observed failure becomes a new repair-lane board task; it
  does not justify slowing every agent pre-emptively. A deploy-dependent task still needs the live
  SHA/canary per `patterns/deploy-contract.md`.
- Research precedes build: capture sources, options, and the chosen path in `PROJECT/research/`
  before architectural work starts. A named technology is a hypothesis, not a mandate.
- Hub reads are unauthenticated by default: publish no topology, credentials, PII, or confidential
  evidence there; add an explicit authentication boundary when the board is private. General writes
  stay token-gated, and that token is terminal board authority — NOT a shell. The hub never
  executes a task's optional verification_command; when one exists, the worker runs it out-of-band
  and submits a typed exit-0 receipt. Strict mode strengthens evidence handling, not the number of
  checks required.
- The board updates at the moment of the event, never batched afterwards. Rendered views of the
  board (the `/hub` dashboard, any generated `TASKS.md`) are projections — change tasks by
  using the served `/hub/api/*` write seam (or `python -m hub_core.client`), never by hand-editing a
  view or opening `EventStore`, `events.jsonl`, or `events.db` from a side process. Direct ledger
  access is offline recovery only because it cannot wake the process serving a Connected cockpit.
- No process-narration comments in code (no directive IDs, no before/after commentary).
